KG42.register("nodes-3659f6083115b4c17437e1c6",{"id":"COMP-259000fdd3b58453","code":"OMC007","label":"Regulatory price-quantity feedback coordination and proposed feed-in premium incentives","definition":"Repeatedly perform regulated price updates, consumer or microgrid response, and dispatch re-solving to coordinate price and quantity; if convergence occurs, then P*=P(Q(P*)) is satisfied. The same unit also contains a regulatory pricing incentive proposal that adds feed-in premiums to the market price.","layer":"L4","template_code":"CBA","concept_id":"coordination.price_signal","concept_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"identity_dimensions":{"identity_fields":["Object: regulated price, energy-use or microgrid decisions, and grid dispatch","Core operation: response, dispatch, and price updates form a feedback loop","Output: a price-quantity configuration satisfying the fixed-point condition upon convergence","Parallel objective: form a microgrid production incentive by adding a premium to the market price"]},"exclusions":["In most coalitions, consumers choose consumption quantities according to price, then dispatch is solved and the regulated price is updated.","In {Private investor, D}, MG and VIU optimize separately: MG takes the regulated price as given, and VIU takes the MG quantity decision as given.","The regulated price of the relevant joint coalition covers only VIU costs; the private investor is not subject to cost-of-service regulation.","The source does not guarantee iterative convergence; feed-in premiums are a proposal, and no specific values or update formula are given."],"members":[{"id":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R001::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","market_meaning":"The utility’s regulated price provides a coordination signal for the microgrid’s optimization of electricity generation, grid imports and reactive power production; the microgrid updates these decisions in response to the resulting regulated price. Scope: Microgrid allocation optimization in Coalition {Private investor, D}, within the iterative utility–microgrid equilibrium calculation. Condition: The utility and microgrid optimize separately; the microgrid treats the utility’s regulated price as fixed during each optimization step. Local allocations must satisfy Kirchhoff’s Current Law at distribution nodes and exclude microgrid exports to the main grid. Distribution expansion enters the microgrid model as a parameter determined by the utility. Scope: Local microgrid allocation model for Coalition {Private investor, D}. Condition: The utility is outside the coalition and determines distribution expansion in its own optimization problem. The allocation model permits generation at distribution nodes and power exports from the microgrid to the rest of the grid, subject to distribution-flow constraints. Scope: Local allocation component of the utility-integrated model for Coalition {VIU, Private investor}, whose distribution constraints are also included in the grand-coalition model. Condition: The microgrid is fully integrated with utility operations.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Local allocation component of the utility-integrated model for Coalition {VIU, Private investor}, whose distribution constraints are also included in the grand-coalition model. | Local microgrid allocation model for Coalition {Private investor, D}. | Microgrid allocation optimization in Coalition {Private investor, D}, within the iterative utility–microgrid equilibrium calculation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e027","v141-b-g04-E12","v141-b-g04-E16","v141-b-g04-E21","v141-b-g04-E25","v141-b-g05-e07"]},"constraint_refs":["16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C004"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"672c93eb7365c2fc0e2e41de93d7deae28cd8e2ec406ced392203b2cc7e80f88","paper":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","rule_ref":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R001","node_id":"coordination.price_signal","function":"coordination.price_signal","unit_id":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-a-M01::coordination.price_signal","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R002::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"The regulated-price signal feeds the microgrid's optimization of generation, imports and reactive power under the coalition's benefit-minus-cost objective; the microgrid updates its decisions using the resulting utility price. Scope: Coalition {Private investor, D}: iterative solution of the microgrid's surplus-maximizing allocation problem alongside the utility's separate optimization. Condition: The utility's regulated price is treated as fixed during each microgrid optimization and updated between iterations. Regulated-price coordination feeds successive solutions of the grand-coalition surplus-maximizing dispatch model: consumers revise their decisions given the price and the model is solved again for optimal power dispatch. Scope: Coalition {VIU, Private investor, D}: regulated-price iteration for the grand-coalition dispatch model. Condition: During successive regulated-price and dispatch iterations. The allocation objective maximizes gross electricity-consumption benefit at the distribution nodes minus generation, power-import, new-capacity and grid-connection costs, subject to distribution-node Kirchhoff's Current Law and microgrid generator and load constraints. Generation costs include consumer expenditures for heat not supplied by microgrid generators. Scope: Coalition {Private investor, D}: joint optimization for the private investor and customers at node D. Condition: Applicable to this coalition's optimization; the utility optimizes separately. The allocation objective maximizes the three players' total surplus subject to network and generator constraints. It includes gross electricity-consumption benefit at distribution nodes and power-sales revenues at all nodes, less generation, reserve, new-capacity, microgrid-connection and depreciation costs and the allowed return on investment. Scope: Coalition {VIU, Private investor, D}: joint optimization of the utility, private investor and customers at node D. Condition: Applicable to the grand-coalition optimization.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Coalition {Private investor, D}: iterative solution of the microgrid's surplus-maximizing allocation problem alongside the utility's separate optimization. | Coalition {Private investor, D}: joint optimization for the private investor and customers at node D. | Coalition {VIU, Private investor, D}: joint optimization of the utility, private investor and customers at node D. | Coalition {VIU, Private investor, D}: regulated-price iteration for the grand-coalition dispatch model.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e027","v141-b-g02-e028","v141-b-g04-E16","v141-b-g04-E24","v141-b-g04-E25"]},"constraint_refs":["16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.social_welfare","source_rule_sha256":"d9adca8c8411923435c363a910af2330dd77280f30f25a8fe012f013fae3a826","paper":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","rule_ref":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R002","node_id":"coordination.price_signal","function":"coordination.price_signal","unit_id":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-a-M01::coordination.price_signal","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"Repeat regulated-price updates and consumers’ responses, with the models solved again for power dispatch, until convergence is achieved. Scope: Numerical equilibrium solution for Coalitions {VIU}, {D}, {VIU, D}, {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D}. Repeat the separate utility–microgrid decision iteration, in which the microgrid updates its decisions in response to the resulting regulated utility price, until convergence is achieved. Scope: Numerical equilibrium solution for Coalition {Private investor, D}, with separate utility and microgrid optimization models. Regulators may introduce feed-in premiums on top of market prices to promote microgrid development and efficient operation, including higher production at peak hours. Scope: Proposed regulatory incentives for microgrid development and efficient operation.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"Numerical equilibrium solution for Coalition {Private investor, D}, with separate utility and microgrid optimization models. | Numerical equilibrium solution for Coalitions {VIU}, {D}, {VIU, D}, {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D}. | Proposed regulatory incentives for microgrid development and efficient operation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e14","v141-b-g05-e06","v141-b-g05-e07"]},"constraint_refs":["16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003"],"decision_anchor":"coordination.price_signal","source_rule_sha256":"3e4e6f71bc46de80f49f5e833b282465778d0474fcf16b7d9cac4bd1044d82f8","paper":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","rule_ref":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003","node_id":"coordination.price_signal","function":"coordination.price_signal","unit_id":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-a-M01::coordination.price_signal","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true}],"paper_ids":["16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E25","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"} { , , } g p [17 recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not sub- ject to c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Solving Coalition {Private investor, D} introduces an additional complexity: the utility and the microgrid take decisions separately, but these decisions influence each other. Their optimization models are solved iteratively. In the first step, given an initial value for the utility’s regulated price, the microgrid optimizes electricity generation, imports from the main grid and reactive power production; the MG treats the utility’s regulated price as fixed. In the second step, the utility optimizes power dispatch in the network, given the microgrid’s quantity decisions; the VIU treats MG exports and generation as fixed. The microgrid successively updates its decisions based on the resulting regulated price for the utility. These steps are repeated until convergence is achieved. Searching for the equilibrium can still be thought of as solving a fixed point problem, where Q^{-1}(P) are the MG’s power imports from the microgrid’s problem. The regulated electricity price depends on Q, the total utility’s sales, that include the microgrid’s imports decisions (Q^{-1}(P)), as well as sales at the other nodes of the network.","paper":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"Solving Coalition {Private investor, D} introduces an additional complexity: the utility and the microgrid take decisions separately, but these decisions influence each other. Their optimization models are solved iteratively. In the first step, given an initial value for the utility’s regulated price, the microgrid optimizes electricity generation, imports from the main grid and reactive power production; the MG treats the utility’s regulated price as fixed. In the second step, the utility optimizes power dispatch in the network, given the microgrid’s quantity decisions; the VIU treats MG exports and generation as fixed. The microgrid successively updates its decisions based on the resulting regulated price for the utility. These steps are repeated until convergence is achieved. Searching for the equilibrium can still be thought of as solving a fixed point problem, where Q^{-1}(P) are the MG’s power imports from the microgrid’s problem. The regulated electricity price depends on Q, the total utility’s sales, that include the microgrid’s imports decisions (Q^{-1}(P)), as well as sales at the other nodes of the network."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e07","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"} { , , } g p [17 recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not sub- ject to c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Solving Coalition {Private investor, D} introduces an additional complexity: the utility and the microgrid take decisions separately, but these decisions influence each other. Their optimization models are solved iteratively. In the first step, given an initial value for the utility’s regulated price, the microgrid optimizes electricity generation, imports from the main grid and reactive power production; the MG treats the utility’s regulated price as fixed. In the second step, the utility optimizes power dispatch in the network, given the microgrid’s quantity decisions; the VIU treats MG exports and generation as fixed. The microgrid successively updates its decisions based on the resulting regulated price for the utility. These steps are repeated until convergence is achieved.","paper":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"Solving Coalition {Private investor, D} introduces an additional complexity: the utility and the microgrid take decisions separately, but these decisions influence each other. Their optimization models are solved iteratively. In the first step, given an initial value for the utility’s regulated price, the microgrid optimizes electricity generation, imports from the main grid and reactive power production; the MG treats the utility’s regulated price as fixed. In the second step, the utility optimizes power dispatch in the network, given the microgrid’s quantity decisions; the VIU treats MG exports and generation as fixed. The microgrid successively updates its decisions based on the resulting regulated price for the utility. These steps are repeated until convergence is achieved."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e05","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"y g y q y sales (Eq. (3)). Thus, ﬁnding the equilibrium price and quantities is a ﬁxed point problem","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Our variable of interest is the regulated electricity price, $P(Q)$. Given a continuous function mapping of $P$ into itself, $P(Q(P))$, the objective is to find a price $P^*$ such that $P^* = P(Q(P^*))$ and we achieve convergence to a fixed point. Convergence is not guaranteed, even if this exists; however, if the procedure converges, it will be to a fixed point. This iterative approach to finding a fixed point equilibrium has worked well in our study.","paper":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"Our variable of interest is the regulated electricity price, $P(Q)$. Given a continuous function mapping of $P$ into itself, $P(Q(P))$, the objective is to find a price $P^*$ such that $P^* = P(Q(P^*))$ and we achieve convergence to a fixed point. Convergence is not guaranteed, even if this exists; however, if the procedure converges, it will be to a fixed point. This iterative approach to finding a fixed point equilibrium has worked well in our study."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e08","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"} { , , } g p [17 recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not sub- ject to c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Searching for the equilibrium can still be thought of as solving a fixed point problem, where $Q_1(P)$ are the MG’s power imports from the microgrid’s problem. The regulated electricity price depends on $Q$, the total utility’s sales, that include the microgrid’s imports decisions ($Q_1(P)$), as well as sales at the other nodes of the network.","paper":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"Searching for the equilibrium can still be thought of as solving a fixed point problem, where $Q_1(P)$ are the MG’s power imports from the microgrid’s problem. The regulated electricity price depends on $Q$, the total utility’s sales, that include the microgrid’s imports decisions ($Q_1(P)$), as well as sales at the other nodes of the network."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e027","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"for its sales to local customers. Given the regulated price, the pri- vate investor can earn a proﬁt","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"For Coalition {Private investor, D}, we present the joint model of the private investor and the consumers at distribution nodes, as well as the model for the utility. The reason for presenting a model for a non-member of the coalition is that, unlike most cooperative game analyses, the value of Coalition {Private investor, D} depends on the utility’s actions. The utility and the microgrid take decisions separately, but each decision influences what the other party does in reaction and affects each other’s surplus; as a result, it is necessary to consider the utility’s problem as well. The goal of the private investor and customers at node D is to maximize the gross benefit of electricity consumption at the distribution nodes, minus costs for generation, power imports, new installed capacity and connection to the grid. The objective function is subject to Kirchhoff’s Current Law at the distribution nodes, plus generator and load constraints in the MG. On the other hand, the utility’s goal is to maximize profits, subject to network and central generation capacity constraints. No power is exported from the MG to the main grid, while distribution expansion represents only a parameter for the MG, as it is determined in the VIU’s problem. Finally, the MG treats the regulated electricity price as fixed, while electricity exports and MG generation are given in the VIU’s problem.","paper":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"For Coalition {Private investor, D}, we present the joint model of the private investor and the consumers at distribution nodes, as well as the model for the utility. The reason for presenting a model for a non-member of the coalition is that, unlike most cooperative game analyses, the value of Coalition {Private investor, D} depends on the utility’s actions. The utility and the microgrid take decisions separately, but each decision influences what the other party does in reaction and affects each other’s surplus; as a result, it is necessary to consider the utility’s problem as well. The goal of the private investor and customers at node D is to maximize the gross benefit of electricity consumption at the distribution nodes, minus costs for generation, power imports, new installed capacity and connection to the grid. The objective function is subject to Kirchhoff’s Current Law at the distribution nodes, plus generator and load constraints in the MG. On the other hand, the utility’s goal is to maximize profits, subject to network and central generation capacity constraints. No power is exported from the MG to the main grid, while distribution expansion represents only a parameter for the MG, as it is determined in the VIU’s problem. Finally, the MG treats the regulated electricity price as fixed, while electricity exports and MG generation are given in the VIU’s problem."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E25","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"} { , , } g p [17 recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not sub- ject to c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Solving Coalition {Private investor, D} introduces an additional complexity: the utility and the microgrid take decisions separately, but these decisions influence each other. Their optimization models are solved iteratively. In the first step, given an initial value for the utility’s regulated price, the microgrid optimizes electricity generation, imports from the main grid and reactive power production; the MG treats the utility’s regulated price as fixed. In the second step, the utility optimizes power dispatch in the network, given the microgrid’s quantity decisions; the VIU treats MG exports and generation as fixed. The microgrid successively updates its decisions based on the resulting regulated price for the utility. These steps are repeated until convergence is achieved. Searching for the equilibrium can still be thought of as solving a fixed point problem, where Q^{-1}(P) are the MG’s power imports from the microgrid’s problem. The regulated electricity price depends on Q, the total utility’s sales, that include the microgrid’s imports decisions (Q^{-1}(P)), as well as sales at the other nodes of the network.","paper":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Solving Coalition {Private investor, D} introduces an additional complexity: the utility and the microgrid take decisions separately, but these decisions influence each other. Their optimization models are solved iteratively. In the first step, given an initial value for the utility’s regulated price, the microgrid optimizes electricity generation, imports from the main grid and reactive power production; the MG treats the utility’s regulated price as fixed. In the second step, the utility optimizes power dispatch in the network, given the microgrid’s quantity decisions; the VIU treats MG exports and generation as fixed. The microgrid successively updates its decisions based on the resulting regulated price for the utility. These steps are repeated until convergence is achieved. Searching for the equilibrium can still be thought of as solving a fixed point problem, where Q^{-1}(P) are the MG’s power imports from the microgrid’s problem. The regulated electricity price depends on Q, the total utility’s sales, that include the microgrid’s imports decisions (Q^{-1}(P)), as well as sales at the other nodes of the network."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e028","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p08-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"hoff’s Current Law at the distribution nodes, plus generator and load constraints in the MG. On the ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The objective of Coalition {VIU, Private investor, D} is to maximize the total surplus of the three players, subject to network and generator constraints. The objective function includes the gross benefit of electricity consumption at the distribution nodes and revenues from power sales at all nodes, minus costs for generation, reserves, new installed capacity and MG connection to the grid, depreciation expenses and allowed return on investment.","paper":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"The objective of Coalition {VIU, Private investor, D} is to maximize the total surplus of the three players, subject to network and generator constraints. The objective function includes the gross benefit of electricity consumption at the distribution nodes and revenues from power sales at all nodes, minus costs for generation, reserves, new installed capacity and MG connection to the grid, depreciation expenses and allowed return on investment."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"y g y q y sales (Eq. (3)). Thus, ﬁnding the equilibrium price and quantities is a ﬁxed point problem","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In Coalitions {VIU}, {D}, {VIU, D}, {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D}, given an initial value for P, customers determine their consumption (equal to the utility’s sales), Q(P), and the model is solved to obtain the optimal power dispatch in the network. Then, a new regulated electricity price, P(Q), is obtained, based on the new level of sales and costs. Given the price, consumers modify their decisions and the models are solved again to find the optimal power dispatch. The last two steps are repeated until convergence is achieved. Note that in Coalitions {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D} the regulated price is set to recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not subject to cost-of-service regulation.","paper":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"In Coalitions {VIU}, {D}, {VIU, D}, {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D}, given an initial value for P, customers determine their consumption (equal to the utility’s sales), Q(P), and the model is solved to obtain the optimal power dispatch in the network. Then, a new regulated electricity price, P(Q), is obtained, based on the new level of sales and costs. Given the price, consumers modify their decisions and the models are solved again to find the optimal power dispatch. The last two steps are repeated until convergence is achieved. Note that in Coalitions {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D} the regulated price is set to recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not subject to cost-of-service regulation."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e06","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"y g y q y sales (Eq. (3)). Thus, ﬁnding the equilibrium price and quantities is a ﬁxed point problem","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In Coalitions {VIU}, {D}, {VIU, D}, {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D}, given an initial value for P, customers determine their consumption (equal to the utility’s sales), Q(P), and the model is solved to obtain the optimal power dispatch in the network. Then, a new regulated electricity price, P(Q), is obtained, based on the new level of sales and costs. Given the price, consumers modify their decisions and the models are solved again to find the optimal power dispatch. The last two steps are repeated until convergence is achieved. Note that in Coalitions {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D} the regulated price is set to recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not subject to cost-of-service regulation.","paper":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"In Coalitions {VIU}, {D}, {VIU, D}, {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D}, given an initial value for P, customers determine their consumption (equal to the utility’s sales), Q(P), and the model is solved to obtain the optimal power dispatch in the network. Then, a new regulated electricity price, P(Q), is obtained, based on the new level of sales and costs. Given the price, consumers modify their decisions and the models are solved again to find the optimal power dispatch. The last two steps are repeated until convergence is achieved. Note that in Coalitions {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D} the regulated price is set to recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not subject to cost-of-service regulation."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e05","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"y g y q y sales (Eq. (3)). Thus, ﬁnding the equilibrium price and quantities is a ﬁxed point problem","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Our variable of interest is the regulated electricity price, $P(Q)$. Given a continuous function mapping of $P$ into itself, $P(Q(P))$, the objective is to find a price $P^*$ such that $P^* = P(Q(P^*))$ and we achieve convergence to a fixed point. Convergence is not guaranteed, even if this exists; however, if the procedure converges, it will be to a fixed point. This iterative approach to finding a fixed point equilibrium has worked well in our study.","paper":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Our variable of interest is the regulated electricity price, $P(Q)$. Given a continuous function mapping of $P$ into itself, $P(Q(P))$, the objective is to find a price $P^*$ such that $P^* = P(Q(P^*))$ and we achieve convergence to a fixed point. Convergence is not guaranteed, even if this exists; however, if the procedure converges, it will be to a fixed point. This iterative approach to finding a fixed point equilibrium has worked well in our study."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e07","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"} { , , } g p [17 recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not sub- ject to c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Solving Coalition {Private investor, D} introduces an additional complexity: the utility and the microgrid take decisions separately, but these decisions influence each other. Their optimization models are solved iteratively. In the first step, given an initial value for the utility’s regulated price, the microgrid optimizes electricity generation, imports from the main grid and reactive power production; the MG treats the utility’s regulated price as fixed. In the second step, the utility optimizes power dispatch in the network, given the microgrid’s quantity decisions; the VIU treats MG exports and generation as fixed. The microgrid successively updates its decisions based on the resulting regulated price for the utility. These steps are repeated until convergence is achieved.","paper":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Solving Coalition {Private investor, D} introduces an additional complexity: the utility and the microgrid take decisions separately, but these decisions influence each other. Their optimization models are solved iteratively. In the first step, given an initial value for the utility’s regulated price, the microgrid optimizes electricity generation, imports from the main grid and reactive power production; the MG treats the utility’s regulated price as fixed. In the second step, the utility optimizes power dispatch in the network, given the microgrid’s quantity decisions; the VIU treats MG exports and generation as fixed. The microgrid successively updates its decisions based on the resulting regulated price for the utility. These steps are repeated until convergence is achieved."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p11-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":11,"printed_page":"534","section":"3.3 Scenario III: tripled MG’s capital costs","anchor_text":"value. sin This scenario is in the lower left corner of Fig. 2, with a negative ach private value an","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-11.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Regulators may introduce incentives (such as feed-in premiums) on top of market prices to promote the MG and its efficient operation (e.g. higher production at peak hours), as recommended by Scheepers et al. [69]. The costs of investing in an increasingly decentralized system may be socialized across all customers in the utility’s rate base. Regulators may also restructure rates to have MGs pay lower connection charges12 and/or demand charge rates, and impose higher remuneration rates for MG services to the grid.\nFinally, utilities may install a meter to measure electricity flows to and from the MG, and offer compensation for net excess generation from the microgrid through net metering, or 'running the meter backward'. The MG would thus effectively be paid the retail price, which includes not only the cost of energy, but also the cost of transmission and distribution. The use of net metering as pricing incentive to promote DG has been criticized, based on the argument that DG providers (and customers) should not receive compensation for distribution services that continue to be provided by the utility and should pay their share of the utility’s fixed costs for maintaining the distribution system [70,71].","paper":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Regulators may introduce incentives (such as feed-in premiums) on top of market prices to promote the MG and its efficient operation (e.g. higher production at peak hours), as recommended by Scheepers et al. [69]. The costs of investing in an increasingly decentralized system may be socialized across all customers in the utility’s rate base. Regulators may also restructure rates to have MGs pay lower connection charges12 and/or demand charge rates, and impose higher remuneration rates for MG services to the grid.\nFinally, utilities may install a meter to measure electricity flows to and from the MG, and offer compensation for net excess generation from the microgrid through net metering, or 'running the meter backward'. The MG would thus effectively be paid the retail price, which includes not only the cost of energy, but also the cost of transmission and distribution. The use of net metering as pricing incentive to promote DG has been criticized, based on the argument that DG providers (and customers) should not receive compensation for distribution services that continue to be provided by the utility and should pay their share of the utility’s fixed costs for maintaining the distribution system [70,71]."}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["price_response_feedback_coordination"],"projection_statuses":["composite"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"kind-L4-9462f7d9b1","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L4","ontological_kind":"composite_rule"},"semantic_definition":{},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-a-M01::coordination.price_signal","paper_id":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"coordination.price_signal","source_occurrences":[{"id":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R001::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","market_meaning":"The utility’s regulated price provides a coordination signal for the microgrid’s optimization of electricity generation, grid imports and reactive power production; the microgrid updates these decisions in response to the resulting regulated price. Scope: Microgrid allocation optimization in Coalition {Private investor, D}, within the iterative utility–microgrid equilibrium calculation. Condition: The utility and microgrid optimize separately; the microgrid treats the utility’s regulated price as fixed during each optimization step. Local allocations must satisfy Kirchhoff’s Current Law at distribution nodes and exclude microgrid exports to the main grid. Distribution expansion enters the microgrid model as a parameter determined by the utility. Scope: Local microgrid allocation model for Coalition {Private investor, D}. Condition: The utility is outside the coalition and determines distribution expansion in its own optimization problem. The allocation model permits generation at distribution nodes and power exports from the microgrid to the rest of the grid, subject to distribution-flow constraints. Scope: Local allocation component of the utility-integrated model for Coalition {VIU, Private investor}, whose distribution constraints are also included in the grand-coalition model. Condition: The microgrid is fully integrated with utility operations.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Local allocation component of the utility-integrated model for Coalition {VIU, Private investor}, whose distribution constraints are also included in the grand-coalition model. | Local microgrid allocation model for Coalition {Private investor, D}. | Microgrid allocation optimization in Coalition {Private investor, D}, within the iterative utility–microgrid equilibrium calculation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e027","v141-b-g04-E12","v141-b-g04-E16","v141-b-g04-E21","v141-b-g04-E25","v141-b-g05-e07"]},"constraint_refs":["16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C004"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"672c93eb7365c2fc0e2e41de93d7deae28cd8e2ec406ced392203b2cc7e80f88"},{"id":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R002::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"The regulated-price signal feeds the microgrid's optimization of generation, imports and reactive power under the coalition's benefit-minus-cost objective; the microgrid updates its decisions using the resulting utility price. Scope: Coalition {Private investor, D}: iterative solution of the microgrid's surplus-maximizing allocation problem alongside the utility's separate optimization. Condition: The utility's regulated price is treated as fixed during each microgrid optimization and updated between iterations. Regulated-price coordination feeds successive solutions of the grand-coalition surplus-maximizing dispatch model: consumers revise their decisions given the price and the model is solved again for optimal power dispatch. Scope: Coalition {VIU, Private investor, D}: regulated-price iteration for the grand-coalition dispatch model. Condition: During successive regulated-price and dispatch iterations. The allocation objective maximizes gross electricity-consumption benefit at the distribution nodes minus generation, power-import, new-capacity and grid-connection costs, subject to distribution-node Kirchhoff's Current Law and microgrid generator and load constraints. Generation costs include consumer expenditures for heat not supplied by microgrid generators. Scope: Coalition {Private investor, D}: joint optimization for the private investor and customers at node D. Condition: Applicable to this coalition's optimization; the utility optimizes separately. The allocation objective maximizes the three players' total surplus subject to network and generator constraints. It includes gross electricity-consumption benefit at distribution nodes and power-sales revenues at all nodes, less generation, reserve, new-capacity, microgrid-connection and depreciation costs and the allowed return on investment. Scope: Coalition {VIU, Private investor, D}: joint optimization of the utility, private investor and customers at node D. Condition: Applicable to the grand-coalition optimization.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Coalition {Private investor, D}: iterative solution of the microgrid's surplus-maximizing allocation problem alongside the utility's separate optimization. | Coalition {Private investor, D}: joint optimization for the private investor and customers at node D. | Coalition {VIU, Private investor, D}: joint optimization of the utility, private investor and customers at node D. | Coalition {VIU, Private investor, D}: regulated-price iteration for the grand-coalition dispatch model.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e027","v141-b-g02-e028","v141-b-g04-E16","v141-b-g04-E24","v141-b-g04-E25"]},"constraint_refs":["16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.social_welfare","source_rule_sha256":"d9adca8c8411923435c363a910af2330dd77280f30f25a8fe012f013fae3a826"},{"id":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"Repeat regulated-price updates and consumers’ responses, with the models solved again for power dispatch, until convergence is achieved. Scope: Numerical equilibrium solution for Coalitions {VIU}, {D}, {VIU, D}, {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D}. Repeat the separate utility–microgrid decision iteration, in which the microgrid updates its decisions in response to the resulting regulated utility price, until convergence is achieved. Scope: Numerical equilibrium solution for Coalition {Private investor, D}, with separate utility and microgrid optimization models. Regulators may introduce feed-in premiums on top of market prices to promote microgrid development and efficient operation, including higher production at peak hours. Scope: Proposed regulatory incentives for microgrid development and efficient operation.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"Numerical equilibrium solution for Coalition {Private investor, D}, with separate utility and microgrid optimization models. | Numerical equilibrium solution for Coalitions {VIU}, {D}, {VIU, D}, {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D}. | Proposed regulatory incentives for microgrid development and efficient operation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e14","v141-b-g05-e06","v141-b-g05-e07"]},"constraint_refs":["16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003"],"decision_anchor":"coordination.price_signal","source_rule_sha256":"3e4e6f71bc46de80f49f5e833b282465778d0474fcf16b7d9cac4bd1044d82f8"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The utility’s regulated price provides a coordination signal for the microgrid’s optimization of electricity generation, grid imports and reactive power production; the microgrid updates these decisions in response to the resulting regulated price. Scope: Microgrid allocation optimization in Coalition {Private investor, D}, within the iterative utility–microgrid equilibrium calculation. Condition: The utility and microgrid optimize separately; the microgrid treats the utility’s regulated price as fixed during each optimization step.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-E25","v141-b-g05-e07","v141-b-g05-e05","v141-b-g05-e08"],"ref":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E25","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"} { , , } g p [17 recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not sub- ject to c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Solving Coalition {Private investor, D} introduces an additional complexity: the utility and the microgrid take decisions separately, but these decisions influence each other. Their optimization models are solved iteratively. In the first step, given an initial value for the utility’s regulated price, the microgrid optimizes electricity generation, imports from the main grid and reactive power production; the MG treats the utility’s regulated price as fixed. In the second step, the utility optimizes power dispatch in the network, given the microgrid’s quantity decisions; the VIU treats MG exports and generation as fixed. The microgrid successively updates its decisions based on the resulting regulated price for the utility. These steps are repeated until convergence is achieved. Searching for the equilibrium can still be thought of as solving a fixed point problem, where Q^{-1}(P) are the MG’s power imports from the microgrid’s problem. The regulated electricity price depends on Q, the total utility’s sales, that include the microgrid’s imports decisions (Q^{-1}(P)), as well as sales at the other nodes of the network."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e07","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"} { , , } g p [17 recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not sub- ject to c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Solving Coalition {Private investor, D} introduces an additional complexity: the utility and the microgrid take decisions separately, but these decisions influence each other. Their optimization models are solved iteratively. In the first step, given an initial value for the utility’s regulated price, the microgrid optimizes electricity generation, imports from the main grid and reactive power production; the MG treats the utility’s regulated price as fixed. In the second step, the utility optimizes power dispatch in the network, given the microgrid’s quantity decisions; the VIU treats MG exports and generation as fixed. The microgrid successively updates its decisions based on the resulting regulated price for the utility. These steps are repeated until convergence is achieved."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e05","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"y g y q y sales (Eq. (3)). Thus, ﬁnding the equilibrium price and quantities is a ﬁxed point problem","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Our variable of interest is the regulated electricity price, $P(Q)$. Given a continuous function mapping of $P$ into itself, $P(Q(P))$, the objective is to find a price $P^*$ such that $P^* = P(Q(P^*))$ and we achieve convergence to a fixed point. Convergence is not guaranteed, even if this exists; however, if the procedure converges, it will be to a fixed point. This iterative approach to finding a fixed point equilibrium has worked well in our study."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e08","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"} { , , } g p [17 recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not sub- ject to c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Searching for the equilibrium can still be thought of as solving a fixed point problem, where $Q_1(P)$ are the MG’s power imports from the microgrid’s problem. The regulated electricity price depends on $Q$, the total utility’s sales, that include the microgrid’s imports decisions ($Q_1(P)$), as well as sales at the other nodes of the network."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C004","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"In the iterative equilibrium calculation for Coalition {Private investor, D}, the regulated price and total utility sales satisfy the fixed-point condition P^* = P(Q(P^*)), where Q includes the microgrid's power imports and sales at the other network nodes. The microgrid treats the regulated price as fixed in its own optimization step.","expression":"P^{*} = P(Q(P^{*}))","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R001-X001","v141-c-R001-X002"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g05-e05","v141-b-g05-e08","v141-b-g04-E25"],"ref":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e05","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"y g y q y sales (Eq. (3)). Thus, ﬁnding the equilibrium price and quantities is a ﬁxed point problem","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Our variable of interest is the regulated electricity price, $P(Q)$. Given a continuous function mapping of $P$ into itself, $P(Q(P))$, the objective is to find a price $P^*$ such that $P^* = P(Q(P^*))$ and we achieve convergence to a fixed point. Convergence is not guaranteed, even if this exists; however, if the procedure converges, it will be to a fixed point. This iterative approach to finding a fixed point equilibrium has worked well in our study."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e08","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"} { , , } g p [17 recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not sub- ject to c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Searching for the equilibrium can still be thought of as solving a fixed point problem, where $Q_1(P)$ are the MG’s power imports from the microgrid’s problem. The regulated electricity price depends on $Q$, the total utility’s sales, that include the microgrid’s imports decisions ($Q_1(P)$), as well as sales at the other nodes of the network."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E25","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"} { , , } g p [17 recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not sub- ject to c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Solving Coalition {Private investor, D} introduces an additional complexity: the utility and the microgrid take decisions separately, but these decisions influence each other. Their optimization models are solved iteratively. In the first step, given an initial value for the utility’s regulated price, the microgrid optimizes electricity generation, imports from the main grid and reactive power production; the MG treats the utility’s regulated price as fixed. In the second step, the utility optimizes power dispatch in the network, given the microgrid’s quantity decisions; the VIU treats MG exports and generation as fixed. The microgrid successively updates its decisions based on the resulting regulated price for the utility. These steps are repeated until convergence is achieved. Searching for the equilibrium can still be thought of as solving a fixed point problem, where Q^{-1}(P) are the MG’s power imports from the microgrid’s problem. The regulated electricity price depends on Q, the total utility’s sales, that include the microgrid’s imports decisions (Q^{-1}(P)), as well as sales at the other nodes of the network."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The regulated-price signal feeds the microgrid's optimization of generation, imports and reactive power under the coalition's benefit-minus-cost objective; the microgrid updates its decisions using the resulting utility price. Scope: Coalition {Private investor, D}: iterative solution of the microgrid's surplus-maximizing allocation problem alongside the utility's separate optimization. Condition: The utility's regulated price is treated as fixed during each microgrid optimization and updated between iterations.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e027","v141-b-g04-E25"],"ref":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e027","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"for its sales to local customers. Given the regulated price, the pri- vate investor can earn a proﬁt","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"For Coalition {Private investor, D}, we present the joint model of the private investor and the consumers at distribution nodes, as well as the model for the utility. The reason for presenting a model for a non-member of the coalition is that, unlike most cooperative game analyses, the value of Coalition {Private investor, D} depends on the utility’s actions. The utility and the microgrid take decisions separately, but each decision influences what the other party does in reaction and affects each other’s surplus; as a result, it is necessary to consider the utility’s problem as well. The goal of the private investor and customers at node D is to maximize the gross benefit of electricity consumption at the distribution nodes, minus costs for generation, power imports, new installed capacity and connection to the grid. The objective function is subject to Kirchhoff’s Current Law at the distribution nodes, plus generator and load constraints in the MG. On the other hand, the utility’s goal is to maximize profits, subject to network and central generation capacity constraints. No power is exported from the MG to the main grid, while distribution expansion represents only a parameter for the MG, as it is determined in the VIU’s problem. Finally, the MG treats the regulated electricity price as fixed, while electricity exports and MG generation are given in the VIU’s problem."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E25","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"} { , , } g p [17 recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not sub- ject to c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Solving Coalition {Private investor, D} introduces an additional complexity: the utility and the microgrid take decisions separately, but these decisions influence each other. Their optimization models are solved iteratively. In the first step, given an initial value for the utility’s regulated price, the microgrid optimizes electricity generation, imports from the main grid and reactive power production; the MG treats the utility’s regulated price as fixed. In the second step, the utility optimizes power dispatch in the network, given the microgrid’s quantity decisions; the VIU treats MG exports and generation as fixed. The microgrid successively updates its decisions based on the resulting regulated price for the utility. These steps are repeated until convergence is achieved. Searching for the equilibrium can still be thought of as solving a fixed point problem, where Q^{-1}(P) are the MG’s power imports from the microgrid’s problem. The regulated electricity price depends on Q, the total utility’s sales, that include the microgrid’s imports decisions (Q^{-1}(P)), as well as sales at the other nodes of the network."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Regulated-price coordination feeds successive solutions of the grand-coalition surplus-maximizing dispatch model: consumers revise their decisions given the price and the model is solved again for optimal power dispatch. Scope: Coalition {VIU, Private investor, D}: regulated-price iteration for the grand-coalition dispatch model. Condition: During successive regulated-price and dispatch iterations.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e028","v141-b-g04-E24"],"ref":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e028","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p08-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"hoff’s Current Law at the distribution nodes, plus generator and load constraints in the MG. On the ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The objective of Coalition {VIU, Private investor, D} is to maximize the total surplus of the three players, subject to network and generator constraints. The objective function includes the gross benefit of electricity consumption at the distribution nodes and revenues from power sales at all nodes, minus costs for generation, reserves, new installed capacity and MG connection to the grid, depreciation expenses and allowed return on investment."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"y g y q y sales (Eq. (3)). Thus, ﬁnding the equilibrium price and quantities is a ﬁxed point problem","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In Coalitions {VIU}, {D}, {VIU, D}, {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D}, given an initial value for P, customers determine their consumption (equal to the utility’s sales), Q(P), and the model is solved to obtain the optimal power dispatch in the network. Then, a new regulated electricity price, P(Q), is obtained, based on the new level of sales and costs. Given the price, consumers modify their decisions and the models are solved again to find the optimal power dispatch. The last two steps are repeated until convergence is achieved. Note that in Coalitions {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D} the regulated price is set to recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not subject to cost-of-service regulation."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Repeat regulated-price updates and consumers’ responses, with the models solved again for power dispatch, until convergence is achieved. Scope: Numerical equilibrium solution for Coalitions {VIU}, {D}, {VIU, D}, {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D}.","expression":"P^{\\star} = P(Q(P^{\\star}))","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003-X001","v141-c-R003-X002"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g05-e06","v141-b-g05-e05"],"ref":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e06","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"y g y q y sales (Eq. (3)). Thus, ﬁnding the equilibrium price and quantities is a ﬁxed point problem","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In Coalitions {VIU}, {D}, {VIU, D}, {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D}, given an initial value for P, customers determine their consumption (equal to the utility’s sales), Q(P), and the model is solved to obtain the optimal power dispatch in the network. Then, a new regulated electricity price, P(Q), is obtained, based on the new level of sales and costs. Given the price, consumers modify their decisions and the models are solved again to find the optimal power dispatch. The last two steps are repeated until convergence is achieved. Note that in Coalitions {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D} the regulated price is set to recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not subject to cost-of-service regulation."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e05","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"y g y q y sales (Eq. (3)). Thus, ﬁnding the equilibrium price and quantities is a ﬁxed point problem","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Our variable of interest is the regulated electricity price, $P(Q)$. Given a continuous function mapping of $P$ into itself, $P(Q(P))$, the objective is to find a price $P^*$ such that $P^* = P(Q(P^*))$ and we achieve convergence to a fixed point. Convergence is not guaranteed, even if this exists; however, if the procedure converges, it will be to a fixed point. This iterative approach to finding a fixed point equilibrium has worked well in our study."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Repeat the separate utility–microgrid decision iteration, in which the microgrid updates its decisions in response to the resulting regulated utility price, until convergence is achieved. Scope: Numerical equilibrium solution for Coalition {Private investor, D}, with separate utility and microgrid optimization models.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g05-e07"],"ref":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e07","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"} { , , } g p [17 recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not sub- ject to c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Solving Coalition {Private investor, D} introduces an additional complexity: the utility and the microgrid take decisions separately, but these decisions influence each other. Their optimization models are solved iteratively. In the first step, given an initial value for the utility’s regulated price, the microgrid optimizes electricity generation, imports from the main grid and reactive power production; the MG treats the utility’s regulated price as fixed. In the second step, the utility optimizes power dispatch in the network, given the microgrid’s quantity decisions; the VIU treats MG exports and generation as fixed. The microgrid successively updates its decisions based on the resulting regulated price for the utility. These steps are repeated until convergence is achieved."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Regulators may introduce feed-in premiums on top of market prices to promote microgrid development and efficient operation, including higher production at peak hours. Scope: Proposed regulatory incentives for microgrid development and efficient operation.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e14"],"ref":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p11-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":11,"printed_page":"534","section":"3.3 Scenario III: tripled MG’s capital costs","anchor_text":"value. sin This scenario is in the lower left corner of Fig. 2, with a negative ach private value an","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-11.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Regulators may introduce incentives (such as feed-in premiums) on top of market prices to promote the MG and its efficient operation (e.g. higher production at peak hours), as recommended by Scheepers et al. [69]. The costs of investing in an increasingly decentralized system may be socialized across all customers in the utility’s rate base. Regulators may also restructure rates to have MGs pay lower connection charges12 and/or demand charge rates, and impose higher remuneration rates for MG services to the grid.\nFinally, utilities may install a meter to measure electricity flows to and from the MG, and offer compensation for net excess generation from the microgrid through net metering, or 'running the meter backward'. The MG would thus effectively be paid the retail price, which includes not only the cost of energy, but also the cost of transmission and distribution. The use of net metering as pricing incentive to promote DG has been criticized, based on the argument that DG providers (and customers) should not receive compensation for distribution services that continue to be provided by the utility and should pay their share of the utility’s fixed costs for maintaining the distribution system [70,71]."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R001-X001","symbol":"P^{*}","meaning":"Regulated electricity price at the fixed-point equilibrium of the iterative utility–microgrid calculation; the microgrid treats the regulated price as fixed in its own optimization step.","kind":"operational","domain":"not specified in source","unit":"$/MW as printed for the regulated price P^e; unit consistency unresolved","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g05-e05","v141-b-g05-e01","v141-b-g04-E25"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R001::x::v141-c-R001-X001"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R001-X002","symbol":"Q","meaning":"Total utility sales, including the microgrid's power imports and sales at other network nodes, used in the regulated-price fixed-point condition.","kind":"operational","domain":"not specified in source","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g05-e08","v141-b-g04-E25","v141-b-g05-e05"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","declaration_ref":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R001::x::v141-c-R001-X002"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X001","symbol":"P","meaning":"Regulated electricity price in the price-signal coordination procedure; the source writes the updated price as P(Q) and the fixed-point price as P*.","kind":"operational","domain":"not specified in source; source treats it as a scalar regulated price","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g05-e05","v141-b-g05-e06"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003-X001"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X002","symbol":"Q","meaning":"Total utility electricity sales, equal to customers' consumption Q(P) in the price-response step of the fixed-point computation.","kind":"operational","domain":"not specified in source; source treats it as a scalar total sales/consumption quantity","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g05-e05","v141-b-g05-e06"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003-X002"}],"parameters":[]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-a-M01::coordination.price_signal","stage":"L4","subtype":"price_response_feedback_coordination","projection_status":"composite","target_definition":"Repeatedly perform regulated price updates, consumer or microgrid response, and dispatch re-solving to coordinate price and quantity; if convergence occurs, then P*=P(Q(P*)) is satisfied. The same unit also contains a regulatory pricing incentive proposal that adds feed-in premiums to the market price.","form_kind":"procedural","identity_fields":["Object: regulated price, energy-use or microgrid decisions, and grid dispatch","Core operation: response, dispatch, and price updates form a feedback loop","Output: a price-quantity configuration satisfying the fixed-point condition upon convergence","Parallel objective: form a microgrid production incentive by adding a premium to the market price"],"variation_fields":["In most coalitions, consumers choose consumption quantities according to price, then dispatch is solved and the regulated price is updated.","In {Private investor, D}, MG and VIU optimize separately: MG takes the regulated price as given, and VIU takes the MG quantity decision as given.","The regulated price of the relevant joint coalition covers only VIU costs; the private investor is not subject to cost-of-service regulation.","The source does not guarantee iterative convergence; feed-in premiums are a proposal, and no specific values or update formula are given."],"constraint_refs":["16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C004","16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003"],"paper_id":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"coordination.price_signal","template_code":"CBA"}],"source_variants":[],"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/37"});
